  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Periods of smoke persist across the region today. Low rain\n   shower and storm chances.\n\n-  Monsoon moisture returns today through Thursday. Localized\n   flash flooding is possible.\n\n-  Hot temperatures are expected this week, with highs reaching\n   the 90s up to 106 degrees in the lower elevations of Idaho.\n\nMostly dry conditions today, with periods of smoke and haze from regional\nwildfires in Oregon and Washington, will degrade air quality and reduce\nvisibility in isolated areas. Today, we'll see the first indications\nof the monsoon moisture, which will be mostly in the form of clouds,\nthough an isolated shower, especially in Lemhi County, is possible.\nThe increased cloud cover will also help with slightly higher \nafternoon humidity values, though elevated fire weather concerns \nwill remain.\n\nStarting Tuesday, a more significant surge of monsoon moisture will arrive\nin the Northern Rockies with 200 to 250 percent of normal moisture content.\nThis moisture, along with an approaching shortwave trough, will be enough\nfor more widespread showers and scattered thunderstorms across western\nMontana and north-central Idaho. These thunderstorms will be capable of\nproducing gusty, erratic winds in excess of 40 mph, frequent lightning, and\nbrief heavy rainfall. If you have plans for outdoor activities, plan\naccordingly each afternoon and evening through Thursday.\n\nWhile we'll experience daily shower and thunderstorm activity this\nweek, we are still looking at above-normal temperatures across \nthe region. Western Montana valleys will see temperatures in the \nmid-90s, with temperatures reaching the low 100s in the Lower \nHells Canyon area of north-central Idaho Wednesday through the \nrest of the week.\n\nBy Friday into the weekend, the apex of the ridge of high pressure will\nmove east over the Northern Plains of the U.S., allowing for increasing\nwesterly winds, drier air, and a decrease in shower activity. This weather\npattern will also reintroduce near-critical fire weather concerns across\nwestern Montana and north-central Idaho.",
